WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.340
Welcome back to Curious Archive.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:13.830
This video is the second half of my two-part
series on the biology of Horizon: Zero Dawn.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:20.840
In the most extreme biomes of this world of
machines, ill-tempered titans, skilled survivalists,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.070
and genuine terrors still await discovery.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:29.699
Like the animals braving the harshest regions
of our current age, these machines have had

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:33.680
to get creative to endure the hazards of the
elements.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.120
The environments are perilous, and the machines
that live here even more so, but sometimes

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.740
discovery requires certain risks.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:47.190
And with mysterious robots that aren’t part
of the ecosystem awakening in the darkest

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.610
corners of the land, who knows what final
secrets are buried beneath the ground.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:01:00.399
So, join me on the last section of our documentary-style
journey, as we conclude our expedition across

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:05.000
the uncharted regions.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.970
In the dense jungles of the far south, a fearsome
storm batters the trees.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:16.910
Typically, I’d wait until such weather passes,
but right now, time is of the essence.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.820
For one of the rarest and most spectacular
machines of all has been spotted here…

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.960
A huge shadow passes overhead.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.460
Above my position is the single mightiest
machine of all the skies.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.460
A Stormbird.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.259
They’re huge — with wingbeats so powerful
they echo like thunderclaps.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:41.080
The Stormbird is not afraid of the hurricane
raging around them.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.760
For this machine is the storm.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.610
And unfortunately for me, these solitary beings
are highly territorial.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:55.110
Soaring close to the ground, the Stormbird
crackles with electrical discharge.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:00.250
At the heart of this machine is what appears
to be an electric field generator, and this

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.250
Stormbird is about to give me a zap.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:06.240
My camera takes the brunt of the blast.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.110
This is why you always bring extra recording
equipment.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.630
Documenting a Stormbird in the middle of a
storm turns out to be an inadvisable idea.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.950
Eventually, however, the storm calms down.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:24.090
And after losing sight of me, the Stormbird
has also mellowed out.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:30.300
In the warm light of dawn, it is easier to
appreciate the majesty of this ruler of the

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.300
skies.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.090
Across the land, there are no machines quite
like them, and our sighting is a promising

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.530
start to the expedition...

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.390
Without the gloom of the storm, the true beauty
of this jungle emerges.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:49.570
With just the right amount of sunlight and
nutrients, this tropical band of forest is

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:50.860
a paradise.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.980
Yet even the lushest gardens have their thorns.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.840
A chill runs down the back of my neck.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.130
A shimmer seems to ripple in the morning light.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.790
I don’t think I’m alone.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.350
Something invisible is moving through the
underbrush.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:12.980
For a brief moment, the camouflage flickers,
and the stealthy machine becomes perceptible.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:19.090
This is a Stalker — a nimble hunter whose
sinewy frame seems designed in every way for

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:20.930
ambush attacks.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:26.630
Their demeanor mirrors that of a large feline
predator, with each measured step hiding the

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.380
incredible speed they’re truly capable of.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:33.510
And the Stalker can take their ambushes to
another level.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:38.850
Through the use of optical camouflage, they
can project the terrain behind them on their

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:39.850
bodies.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:45.650
This form of invisibility is almost perfect,
with only a slight bend in the light suggesting

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.709
the Stalker’s outline.

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:53.280
And as the Stalker moves, they leaves behind
small proximity mines that will detonate if

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.500
I get too close, alerting the Stalker to my
presence.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:02.660
Trying to keep one eye where I’m stepping
and one eye on the underbrush is harrowing.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:08.030
Yet I have discovered a single weakness — in
order to take in light, the Stalker’s optical

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.030
sensors must remain visible.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:15.360
If I keep watch for these floating dots, I
should be able to anticipate attacks.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.190
Yet knowing an attack is coming is one thing.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.280
Surviving an attack is another.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.500
One Stalker has evidently decided they’ve
waited enough, and goes on the offensive.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.780
Uncloaking, they take a hurried swipe at my
head.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.840
A close call.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.170
Every part of the Stalker is built for combat,
from the metal claws and teeth of their front,

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.030
to the stinger-like blade at the end of their
tail.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:43.000
I doubt this is a fight I can win.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.690
Yet just as quickly as the Stalker appears,
they vanish, disappearing once more to wait

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.160
for their next opportunity.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.130
I’m not keen on another surprise attack,
and decide to continue my expedition in a

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:00.320
region with a thinner underbrush…

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.180
I travel to the north, a region where little
vegetation grows.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:10.150
Here, a vast stone quarry stretches along
the length of a canyon.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:14.710
This colossal excavation project appears long
abandoned.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:18.120
Yet somehow, the site still feels occupied…

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.870
A terrible rumbling echoes through the quarry.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.970
Something is moving under the surface of the
rock.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:31.270
For a brief moment, a gigantic machine breaches
from the sand like a surfacing whale.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:37.180
Stumbling backwards, I witness the metal goliath
rise up and clamp their jaws where I had just

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.230
been standing, before diving once again.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:42.960
That was a Rockbreaker.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:49.150
One of the largest known machines, these agents
of destruction can shred through any material.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:54.340
Like a sentient excavation drill, they use
this ability to ‘swim’ under the Earth’s

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.050
crust as a fish moves through water.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:02.800
When they detect a sound, they move, shark-like,
to the source of the noise, and attack the

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.460
unlucky target from below.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:10.900
If a target is difficult to apprehend, Rockbreakers
will briefly surface — giving us a chance

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.770
to look more closely at their anatomy.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:20.550
While they might function like a fish, Rockbreakers
possess powerful limbs that resemble an excavator’s

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:21.550
shovel.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:26.210
Tunneling animals like badgers or moles have
similar digging mitts that they use to create

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:27.770
their burrows.

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:33.550
The most prominent tunneling tool of the Rockbreaker,
though, is their massive mouth, composed of

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.389
an enormous roller-cone drill bit.

00:06:36.389 --> 00:06:42.479
With this feature, a Rockbreaker can swallow
vast quantities of raw material each day.

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:48.760
Yet what goes down must come up, and the Rockbreaker
needs to regularly regurgitate processed stones

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:52.860
into the air, which rain down upon the surface.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.229
With a Rockbreaker on the loose, this quarry
is as perilous as it gets.

00:06:58.229 --> 00:07:03.550
Time to tiptoe away to our next location…

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:09.680
In the western desert, I spot a herd of antelope-like
creatures racing over the dunes.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.919
These are Lancehorns, and while I only catch
a glimpse of them, they look majestic.

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:16.970
Yet they are clearly in a hurry...

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.400
And I soon find out why.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:24.350
Out of nowhere, a heavy sandstorm sweeps over
the western reaches.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:30.270
Stumbling through the storm, I find various
familiar machines are also struggling to press

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:33.130
on against the howling winds.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:38.800
All any of us can do is keep moving and hope
the storm dies down.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.190
But there is something in this cloud more
dangerous than the elements.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:47.010
Stalking within the sandstorm is a terror
from a lost age.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:48.010
A Corruptor.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:53.470
Moving on four, scorpion-like legs, this machine
isn’t like the others I’ve seen.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:59.570
Not a part of the larger machine ecosystem,
they possess no behavior beyond unreasoning

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.070
hostility.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.110
Yet how did such a machine come to be?

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:09.770
This stretch of desert, called The Rustwatch,
is scattered with mysterious ruins.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:15.610
It seems a battle was waged here long ago,
with many dormant machines of war now rusting

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.759
away in the sand.

00:08:17.759 --> 00:08:22.919
Perhaps this Corruptor is the last one still
functioning — a thoughtless agent of war

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:26.759
from a conflict that ended a millennia ago.

00:08:26.759 --> 00:08:31.740
In any case, I have a more current problem
— the Corruptor has spotted me.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:37.289
Yet to my surprise, before the Corruptor can
strike, other machines descend upon it from

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:41.599
all directions, unleashing every attack in
their arsenal.

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:47.559
Like immune-system cells descending upon an
infection, the other machines seem to instinctually

00:08:47.559 --> 00:08:50.490
see the Corruptor as a threat to the ecosystem.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:56.579
It’s a surprising turn, but not one I wish
to dwell on, as the ensuing chaos has given

00:08:56.579 --> 00:09:00.540
me the perfect opportunity to make a hasty
exit.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.130
Yet while I leave the Rustwatch, I can’t
help but wonder if there are other long-lost

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:10.699
machines hidden away in the margins of this
world…

00:09:10.699 --> 00:09:15.490
With the western desert and southern jungle
now charted, the greatest frontier of all

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:16.699
still remains.

00:09:16.699 --> 00:09:22.459
Far to the north, the Frozen Wilds — an
immense mountainous region — beckon with

00:09:22.459 --> 00:09:25.660
the promise of untouched wilderness.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:30.790
Temperatures drop low in the forests and mountains
of these wilds, with the wind carrying cold

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:33.149
drafts of snow between the trees.

00:09:33.149 --> 00:09:36.189
It’s a good thing I’ve come with layers…

00:09:36.189 --> 00:09:40.480
Yet some denizens of this mountainous forest
don’t have to bundle up.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:44.740
Rushing ahead of me is a herd of remarkable
machines.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:45.920
Chargers.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.759
These swift-footed beings are the champions
of the freezing alpine slopes, and are comfortable

00:09:51.759 --> 00:09:55.619
on inclines most creatures wouldn’t dare
climb.

00:09:55.619 --> 00:10:00.649
In behavior and general appearance, Chargers
are perhaps most similar to mountain goats.

00:10:00.649 --> 00:10:05.779
Curiously, there are organic mountain goats
present in this region, in a rare instance

00:10:05.779 --> 00:10:09.699
of machines alongside the animal whose role
they fill.

00:10:09.699 --> 00:10:15.699
As herbivorous machines, Chargers spend much
of their time seeking out patches of vegetation.

00:10:15.699 --> 00:10:20.930
Unlike some herbivores, though, Chargers have
an aggressive side.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:26.639
When I step too close, the animals rush towards
me, kicking wildly to scare me off.

00:10:26.639 --> 00:10:30.529
I certainly now understand why these animals
are called ‘Chargers.’

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:39.089
It’s a lesson I’ll be sure to remember:
better leave these slopes to the Chargers.

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:45.429
Moving to the valley below, I discover a landscape
of surreal, prismatic hot springs.

00:10:45.429 --> 00:10:50.910
These natural springs exist in the modern
day, and tells me this is a region we would

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:54.040
know as Yellowstone National Park.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:59.540
The bright, vivid colors in the spring are
the result of microbial mats around the edges

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.939
of the mineral-rich water.

00:11:01.939 --> 00:11:06.639
And tromping at the water’s edge is a herd
of bellowing giants.

00:11:06.639 --> 00:11:10.470
These huge, stocky machines are known as Tramplers.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:15.949
With their massive head, neck and chest, Tramplers
look like no force on Earth could knock them

00:11:15.949 --> 00:11:16.949
down.

00:11:16.949 --> 00:11:22.290
With so much bulk, they seem to be less bothered
by the falling temperatures than I am.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:27.720
Like the Bison of North America, Tramplers
are highly resistant to the wind and snow.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:34.410
And for any non-elemental challenges, a pair
of huge hornlike structures keep the Tramplers

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.329
prepared for anything.

00:11:36.329 --> 00:11:40.249
Tramplers also use these horns to dig up rock
and soil.

00:11:40.249 --> 00:11:45.489
They work tirelessly, stopping only to change
location to another spot.

00:11:45.489 --> 00:11:47.249
But for what purpose?

00:11:47.249 --> 00:11:53.480
Soil analysis suggests that Tramplers are,
in fact, purifying the toxin-laden soil of

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.480
this land.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.190
They filter the material they dig up through
a processing unit on their underside, which

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:03.540
glows with heat from its arduous task.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:09.959
With each region the Tramplers pass through,
the immediate ecosystem becomes a little healthier.

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:12.720
But Tramplers aren’t always peaceful.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.230
Highly territorial, these bulls will charge
interlopers with unpredictable speed.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:25.240
If pushed, Tramplers will even use their processing
units to set the environment ablaze, scorching

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:27.929
the land they typically protect.

00:12:27.929 --> 00:12:33.699
Wanting to avoid such an outcome, I stay out
of range of the herd’s optical sensors,

00:12:33.699 --> 00:12:38.129
and watch them pass on…

00:12:38.129 --> 00:12:42.629
Cresting the next hill, I witness a Tallneck
trudging through the snow.

00:12:42.629 --> 00:12:48.079
It’s surprising to see one this far north,
but it seems not even the cold can slow these

00:12:48.079 --> 00:12:49.230
beings down.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:54.699
Or the trees, for that matter, which this
Tallneck seems to take little notice of.

00:12:54.699 --> 00:13:00.800
I find it is comforting to see a familiar
machine in such an unfamiliar land…

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:05.860
Yet just ahead looms a machine that is distinctly
unfamiliar.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:12.119
These are Control Towers, and unlike all other
types of machines, they look botanical rather

00:13:12.119 --> 00:13:13.119
than zoological.

00:13:13.119 --> 00:13:19.519
To my astonishment, the strange machine then
releases a cloud of spiraling drones into

00:13:19.519 --> 00:13:23.730
the air that burst with bizarre, purple energy.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:29.730
As it turns out, Control Towers act as the
unofficial hearts of the synthetic ecosystem,

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:33.170
with their drones helping to repair nearby
machines.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:38.449
Their bizarre appearance is a result of this
peculiar, sessile lifestyle.

00:13:38.449 --> 00:13:44.799
Why bother with locomotion, when your legion
of loyal drones can go anywhere you can’t?

00:13:44.799 --> 00:13:49.190
While the undulating movements of these towers
subtly resemble the motion of coral, more

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:53.060
than any other machine I’ve documented,
Control Towers have no clear natural analogues.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:58.230
Yet in repairing damaged machines, the importance
of their role in the continued health of the

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.509
machine ecosystem cannot be overstated.

00:14:00.509 --> 00:14:06.649
And with such a vital occupation, Control
Towers are fiercely defended by the machines

00:14:06.649 --> 00:14:07.690
they repair.

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:12.920
On that note, I think I’ve gotten close
enough already...

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:16.329
To the north, the snowfall has become heavier.

00:14:16.329 --> 00:14:21.160
Like the sandstorm in the western desert,
a blizzard descends over this region in a

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:22.970
matter of minutes.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:28.139
Even with my layers of protection, I’ll
need to find shelter if I am to survive this

00:14:28.139 --> 00:14:29.259
onslaught.

00:14:29.259 --> 00:14:34.589
In the distance, I spot a cave illuminated
with the artificial lights of the hunter-gatherer

00:14:34.589 --> 00:14:37.929
communities who occupy this region.

00:14:37.929 --> 00:14:40.629
Perhaps refuge awaits inside?

00:14:40.629 --> 00:14:42.180
No such luck.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.560
From the cave emerges a fiery machine.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:51.379
This is a Scorcher, and they have taken over
this cave from its previous occupants.

00:14:51.379 --> 00:14:56.790
Their tall pointed ears give them a vulpine
appearance, although Scorchers seems far less

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:58.089
skittish.

00:14:58.089 --> 00:15:01.970
I suspect the snow is now the least of my
worries.

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:07.399
Waiting for an attack, I watch the Scorcher
crouch down — then rocket forwards, leaving

00:15:07.399 --> 00:15:08.850
a blazing trail.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:10.470
A near hit.

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:16.149
Despite living in a cold environment, the
Scorcher is an agent of the inferno, creating

00:15:16.149 --> 00:15:19.819
devastating, if somewhat inaccurate walls
of fire.

00:15:19.819 --> 00:15:23.559
I might be cold, but this is no way to warm
up.

00:15:23.559 --> 00:15:26.609
Time to find a different shelter.

00:15:26.609 --> 00:15:31.639
Stumbling through the storm, I happen at last
upon a snow-covered ruin.

00:15:31.639 --> 00:15:36.109
The structure has seen better days, but I’ll
have to take my chances.

00:15:36.109 --> 00:15:40.691
The inside is thankfully much warmer… but
what is this a ruin of?

00:15:40.691 --> 00:15:46.209
A collection of forgotten animal statues in
the corner gives me a clue.

00:15:46.209 --> 00:15:50.910
I’m standing in the ruins of the Yellowstone
National Park Visitor Center.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:56.929
And these statues are, sadly, the last record
of their respective species.

00:15:56.929 --> 00:16:01.779
Once this building falls, these animals will
be lost to time.

00:16:01.779 --> 00:16:06.459
Through a crack in the wall, I notice the
snow outside has calmed down.

00:16:06.459 --> 00:16:11.839
The time has come to complete the final leg
of my journey…

00:16:11.839 --> 00:16:15.829
At the foot of the mountains rises a fortress-like
ruin.

00:16:15.829 --> 00:16:22.160
This was once a hydroelectric dam, through
which great quantities of water still flow.

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:27.919
And stalking the base of this dam is one of
the most dangerous machine of the frozen wilds.

00:16:27.919 --> 00:16:29.899
A Frostclaw.

00:16:29.899 --> 00:16:36.189
Like polar bears, Frostclaws have muscular
builds and are practically immune to the cold.

00:16:36.189 --> 00:16:40.189
Indeed, Frostclaws are champions of the ice.

00:16:40.189 --> 00:16:46.319
Using a chemical similar to liquid nitrogen,
Frostclaws can create sharp spires of frozen

00:16:46.319 --> 00:16:52.239
coolant — transforming the surrounding area
into a forest of jagged ice.

00:16:52.239 --> 00:16:55.990
Much of the dam is covered with these glacial
stalagmites.

00:16:55.990 --> 00:16:58.291
And this Frostclaw is not alone.

00:16:58.291 --> 00:17:03.119
A Fireclaw has also made their home on this
dam.

00:17:03.119 --> 00:17:07.189
This infernal machine is even larger than
their icy cousin.

00:17:07.189 --> 00:17:10.710
And they are known to be even more aggressive.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:16.459
The Frostclaws’ opposite, Fireclaws wield
flames much like Scorchers.

00:17:16.459 --> 00:17:22.070
Frost and Fireclaws might not seem like they’d
get along, but surprisingly, these two seem

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:23.830
to tolerate each other.

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:30.570
With both a Frostclaw and a Fireclaw at this
site, close documentation is a serious risk.

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:35.080
Fortunately, the Dam’s ruins provide reliable
cover.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:39.800
All I have to do is find a space too narrow
for the machines to fit through, and stay

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:44.790
hidden until the coast is clear….

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:50.320
At the very edge of the world, the final stop
on our journey is a bleak graveyard.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:55.260
Like the wreckage of the Rustwatch, this seems
to have been the site of a great battle.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:18:02.290
Yet who — or what — were the ancient humans
fighting against when civilization fell?

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:07.160
Tromping through the snow is a metal monster
unlike any seen before.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:13.710
This ancient horror is a Deathbringer, the
last machine standing in this grim cemetery.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:18.850
Like the smaller Corruptor I encountered in
the sands, the Deathbringer has no resemblance

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:23.260
to any fauna, and is simply a unit of destruction.

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:29.900
Many of its weapon systems are no longer functioning,
but it seethes with blind hate nonetheless.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:34.310
Perhaps it was rogue units like this one that
were the cause of humanity’s collapse.

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:39.240
Or perhaps the Deathbringers were aided by
something much bigger.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:45.060
Throughout my travels, I’ve witnessed many
immense metal tentacles snaking over the mountains.

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:51.510
These machines, known simply as Metal Devils,
have been in the back of my mind this entire

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:52.740
voyage.

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:57.970
No still functioning-units of this machine
are known to exist, but their proximity to

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:00.730
ruined battlefields raise suspicion.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:06.620
I suspect these machines are responsible for
the doom of the previous ecosystem, and the

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:12.230
animal machines that populate this land were
built to return the world to a habitable state.

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:22.170
In time, these ruins will hopefully be forgotten,
and Earth will become the planet it once was...

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:27.020
Warming up in the temperate safety of the
Embrace, I consider all I’ve seen in this

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:28.730
strange future.

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:34.050
While a quiet sadness permeates throughout
the ruins of this world, the interconnected

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:39.590
machines that populate the land have given
the planet new life — so to speak.

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:44.990
I’ve heard rumors of a vastly different
ecosystem of machines in a region known as

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:50.750
the Forbidden West, and if passage to that
location is ever possible, perhaps someday

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:52.450
I’ll chart it as well.

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:57.110
But for now, the time has come for to return
home...

00:19:57.110 --> 00:19:59.550
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:04.490
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:09.070
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:27.130
See you in the next video.

